WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.459 --> 00:00:04.520
Dear Fellow Scholars, this is Two Minute Papers
with Károly Zsolnai-Fehér.

00:00:04.520 --> 00:00:09.960
This is a contest by OpenAI where a bunch
of AIs compete to decide who has the best

00:00:09.960 --> 00:00:12.269
transfer learning capabilities.

00:00:12.269 --> 00:00:17.220
Transfer learning means that the training
and the testing environments differ significantly,

00:00:17.220 --> 00:00:22.109
therefore only the AIs that learn general
concepts prevail, and the ones that try to

00:00:22.109 --> 00:00:25.550
get by with memorizing things will quickly
fall.

00:00:25.550 --> 00:00:30.279
In this experiment, these programs start playing
Sonic the Hedgehog, and are given a bunch

00:00:30.279 --> 00:00:31.909
of levels to train on.

00:00:31.909 --> 00:00:38.059
However, like in a good test at school, the
levels for the final evaluation are kept secret!

00:00:38.059 --> 00:00:43.890
So, the goal is that only high-quality general
algorithms prevail and we can't cheat through

00:00:43.890 --> 00:00:47.480
the program as we don't know what the final
exam will entail.

00:00:47.480 --> 00:00:51.530
We only know that we have to make the most
of the training materials to pass.

00:00:51.530 --> 00:00:56.989
Sonic is a legendary platform game where we
have to blaze through levels by avoiding obstacles

00:00:56.989 --> 00:01:00.899
and traps often while traveling with the speed
of sound.

00:01:00.899 --> 00:01:05.530
Here you can see the winning submission taking
the exam on a previously unseen level.

00:01:05.530 --> 00:01:10.560
After one minute of training, as expected,
the AI started to explore the controls, but

00:01:10.560 --> 00:01:14.750
is still quite inept and does not make any
meaningful progress on the level.

00:01:14.750 --> 00:01:20.420
After 30 minutes, things look significantly
better as the AI now understands the basics

00:01:20.420 --> 00:01:21.560
of the game.

00:01:21.560 --> 00:01:27.620
And look here, almost got up there, and, got
it.

00:01:27.620 --> 00:01:36.200
It is clearly making progress as it collects
some coins, defeats enemies, goes through

00:01:36.200 --> 00:01:42.619
the loop, and gets stuck seemingly because
it doesn't yet know how being under water

00:01:42.619 --> 00:01:44.680
changes how high it can jump.

00:01:44.680 --> 00:01:48.329
This is quite a bit of a special case, so,
we are getting there.

00:01:48.329 --> 00:01:55.259
After only 60-120 minutes, it became a competent
player and was able to finish this challenging

00:01:55.259 --> 00:01:57.820
map with only a few mistakes.

00:01:57.820 --> 00:02:01.369
Really impressive transfer learning in just
about an hour.

00:02:01.369 --> 00:02:19.800
And note that the algorithm has never seen
this level before.

00:02:19.800 --> 00:02:24.540
Here you see a really cool visualization of
three different AI's progress on the map,

00:02:24.540 --> 00:02:29.250
where the red dots indicate the movement of
the character for earlier episodes, and the

00:02:29.250 --> 00:02:32.940
bluer colors show the progress at later stages
of the training.

00:02:32.940 --> 00:02:36.170
I could spend all day staring at these.

00:02:36.170 --> 00:02:40.350
Videos are available for many many submissions,
some of which even opened up their source

00:02:40.350 --> 00:02:45.280
code and there are a few high-quality write-ups
as well, so make sure to have a look!

00:02:45.280 --> 00:02:47.810
There's gonna be lots of fun to be had there.

00:02:47.810 --> 00:02:52.661
This competition gives us something that is
scientifically interesting, practical, and

00:02:52.661 --> 00:02:54.990
super fun at the same time.

00:02:54.990 --> 00:02:56.760
What more could you possibly want?

00:02:56.760 --> 00:03:01.940
Huge thumbs up for the OpenAI team for organizing
this, and of course, congratulations to the

00:03:01.940 --> 00:03:02.990
participants.

00:03:02.990 --> 00:03:07.080
And now you see that we have a job where we
train computers to play video games, and we

00:03:07.080 --> 00:03:08.900
are even paid for it.

00:03:08.900 --> 00:03:09.900
What a time to be alive.

00:03:09.900 --> 00:03:14.900
By the way, if you wish to unleash the inner
scholar in you, Two Minute Papers shirts are

00:03:14.900 --> 00:03:17.320
available in many sizes and colors.

00:03:17.320 --> 00:03:18.360
We have mugs too!

00:03:18.360 --> 00:03:20.520
The links are available in the video description.

00:03:20.520 --> 00:03:24.340
Thanks for watching and for your generous
support, and I'll see you next time!

